data.content:
Yes — MSTest v3.7+ (mid-2025) introduced **`[TestDataRow<T>]`** for inline typed data, eliminating `object[]` entirely:

```csharp
[TestMethod]
[TestDataRow<string, bool>("valid@email.com", true)]
[TestDataRow<string, bool>("invalid", false)]
[TestDataRow<string, bool>("", false)]
public void Validate_ReturnsExpected(string input, bool expected)
{
    var result = Validator.IsValid(input);
    Assert.AreEqual(expected, result);
}
```

If you prefer a data source method, you can use **typed tuples** with `DynamicData`:

```csharp
[TestMethod]
[DynamicData(nameof(TestData))]
public void Validate_ReturnsExpected(string input, bool expected)
{
    var result = Validator.IsValid(input);
    Assert.AreEqual(expected, result);
}

public static IEnumerable<(string Input, bool Expected)> TestData =>
[
    ("valid@email.com", true),
    ("invalid", false),
    ("", false),
];
```

**Key differences:**
- **`TestDataRow<T>`** — inline, fully type-checked at compile time (replaces `[DataRow]`)
- **Tuple-based `DynamicData`** — type-safe and cleaner when you have many rows or computed data

Both require **MSTest 3.7+** (`Microsoft.Testing.Framework`). Update with:
```
dotnet add package MSTest --version 3.7.0
```
